# I²C-Based Health Monitoring Node for Pulse and Temperature Acquisition

## Aim
To develop an embedded health monitoring node that acquires pulse rate and temperature readings using sensors and I²C communication.

## Components Required
- Arduino UNO
- Pulse Sensor (Analog Pulse Sensor)
- LM75 I²C Temperature Sensor
- Jumper Wires
- Breadboard
- USB Cable

## Software Required
- Arduino IDE

## Description
This project demonstrates a health monitoring system for acquiring pulse rate and temperature readings.

- **Pulse Sensor:** Detects heartbeat signals and estimates pulse rate in beats per minute (BPM).
- **LM75 Temperature Sensor:** Measures temperature and communicates with Arduino UNO through the I²C protocol.
- **Arduino UNO:** Processes sensor readings and displays the measured values through the Serial Monitor.

## Pin Connections

| Component | Arduino UNO Pin |
|---|---|
| Pulse Sensor Signal | A0 |
| LM75 SDA | A4 |
| LM75 SCL | A5 |
| LM75 VCC | 5V |
| LM75 GND | GND |
| Pulse Sensor VCC | 5V |
| Pulse Sensor GND | GND |

## Working Principle
1. The pulse sensor detects heartbeat signals.
2. Arduino processes the pulse signals to estimate BPM.
3. The LM75 sensor measures temperature.
4. Temperature data is transferred through the I²C communication protocol.
5. Arduino displays pulse rate and temperature readings on the Serial Monitor.

## Procedure
1. Connect the pulse sensor to analog pin A0.
2. Connect the LM75 sensor to the I²C pins A4 and A5.
3. Open Arduino IDE.
4. Select Arduino UNO and the appropriate COM port.
5. Upload the program.
6. Open the Serial Monitor at 9600 baud.
7. Observe pulse rate and temperature readings.

## Expected Output
The Serial Monitor displays:
- Pulse rate in BPM.
- Temperature in degrees Celsius.
- Continuous sensor readings.

## Result
Thus, an I²C-based health monitoring node for pulse and temperature acquisition was implemented using Arduino UNO.

## Applications
- Educational health monitoring prototypes
- Embedded sensor interfacing
- Temperature monitoring
- Pulse rate measurement

## Note
This is an educational prototype and is not a medical diagnostic device. Pulse readings depend on sensor placement and signal quality.
